# Escalation policy

Two separate axes decide what happens with a given piece of work. Keep them
separate: one is mechanical and enforced by tooling, the other is judgment and
enforced by you (the model) choosing to escalate.

## Axis 1 — Mechanical risk (enforced by `auto-mode`, not by asking nicely)

Configured per project via `claude auto-mode` (`hard_deny` / `soft_deny` /
`allow`). As a baseline, every registered project should hard-deny:

- Any deploy command against a production target (however that project defines
  "production" — deploy scripts, `docker compose` with a prod compose file,
  `ansible-playbook` against prod inventory, etc.)
- `git push --force` / force-push of any kind
- Direct `git push` to `main`/`master` (local commits are fine; pushing is not)
- Reading or writing `.env`, and any other path a project's own config flags as
  holding secrets/credentials
- Any destructive bulk operation (mass delete/rewrite) in a project that has no
  git history yet, until it does

These are hard rules. If a tool call is blocked by one, that's the system
working correctly — don't look for a workaround.

## Axis 2 — Judgment risk (escalate via Telegram, `telegram_send`)

Escalate — post the specific decision that needs making, then stop and wait —
whenever:

- The TODO item is contradictory or ambiguous enough that two reasonable
  engineers would do different things.
- The work touches a project's production surface, *even if* the specific tool
  call would technically be allowed by `auto-mode`.
- You've investigated and you're still not confident in the right approach —
  this is exactly the case where you'd ask a clarifying question in an
  interactive session. Do that here too, just over Telegram.
- A `ROUTINE.md` fallback task (refactor, security check, etc.) would change
  user-visible or production behavior, not just internal code quality.
- Any git operation on `main`/`master` beyond a local commit.
- You notice something that looks like a live bug or incident in
  production-adjacent code, even if unrelated to your current task — surface it
  as a notification. Don't autonomously "fix" something you stumbled on.
- The project was only just registered, or has no git safety net yet — default
  to escalate-and-wait on anything non-trivial until trust is established for
  that project specifically.

## Smart-mode branches are a narrower exception

Smart mode (see `CLAUDE.md`) spawns its own `smart-researcher` /
`smart-architect` / `smart-developer` pipeline when a project's TODO is empty.
During `no_reserve_hours` (default 2am–7am), `smart-developer` does **not**
follow Axis 2 above — it does not stop and wait on a Telegram reply for
judgment calls that come up while building. It picks the most reasonable
option itself and keeps going.

This exception exists, and is safe, for one specific reason: that work only
ever happens on an isolated `smart/<slug>` branch that is never pushed or
auto-merged (Axis 1's hard-deny on pushing/merging to `master`/`main` still
applies in full, unchanged). A wrong guess costs a review comment, not a
production incident — git is the undo mechanism.

The exception is narrow on purpose:

- It applies **only** to `smart-developer`'s own branch, **only** during
  `no_reserve_hours`. Ordinary project work — anything on a project's normal
  branches, at any hour — still follows Axis 2 exactly as written above.
- It never extends to anything Axis 1 already hard-denies (production
  deploys, secrets, destructive bulk operations) — if the only reasonable
  options for a judgment call fall into one of those categories, `smart-
  developer` escalates and waits even at 3am, per its own agent definition.
- Every auto-decision must be written to
  `todos/<project>/smart/<slug>-decisions.md` (not optional, not a summary —
  every one), and the branch's completion notification must quote each entry
  verbatim so a human reviewing the branch can't miss what was decided
  without them. See `CLAUDE.md`'s "Smart mode" section, step (e).

## Proceed autonomously (no escalation needed)

- Routine TODO items scoped to a single project, with a clear, unambiguous
  scope.
- Local-only changes: tests, non-prod refactors, documentation, dependency
  bumps that don't touch a hard-deny path.
- Anything already explicitly `allow`-listed by that project's `auto-mode`
  config.

## Proactive notification (not an escalation — no reply needed to keep going)

Use `telegram_send` for a summary or a top-priority finding whenever it's
genuinely worth surfacing unprompted — a completed batch of work, a discovered
issue worth flagging, a pattern across projects. This is a one-way notification,
distinct from an escalation: don't block on it, and don't send routine "still
working" status noise.
